{
  "_comment": "Field mapping config for AlertForge's log parser. Each profile is checked in order; the first profile whose 'detect_keys' are all found (case-insensitive, anywhere in the flattened log) wins. 'fields' map field names to a dot-path into the JSON log. Use \"literal:Some Text\" for a fixed value, or \"path::transform\" to apply a transform function (see TRANSFORMS in parser_core.py). Path syntax also supports 'field[0]' (list index) and 'field[Name=Value]' (find a {\"Name\":..,\"Value\":..} pair in a list by name - common in O365/Rapid7 audit logs). A field spec can also chain '||'-separated fallback candidates (tried in order, first non-empty wins) and can be prefixed 'all:path' to aggregate that value across EVERY pasted JSON record instead of just the first - useful when an analyst pastes one JSON object per related event (e.g. one per file copied to USB). Transforms include flatten_name_value_pairs (a real nested Name/Value array -> 'Name=Value; ...' string), flatten_name_value_json_string (same, but for a field that holds a JSON-encoded STRING of that array, e.g. Sentinel OfficeActivity's Parameters field), and join_list (join a list value into 'a; b; c', passing scalars through unchanged - e.g. Sentinel watchlist alerts where Computer/IpAddressList are arrays). The first 15 fields of every profile below (user..device_os) are the common cross-source set so generic escalation templates using {user}/{host}/{process}/etc. work no matter which log type was parsed; everything after that is source-specific bonus detail (geoip_*, device_*, hashes, rule parameters, etc.).",
